---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout title="Phil Ball, PhD — Strategic economist" description="Strategic economist working across public value, investment, place, resilience and institutional decision-making">
  <div class="home-shell">
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <span class="eyebrow">Strategic economist · London</span>
        <h1>Better decisions<br/><em>under uncertainty.</em></h1>
        <p>My work focuses on how institutions allocate resources, navigate trade-offs and turn expected benefits into realised public value.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="#work">Explore selected work</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={`${base}projects/`}>View projects</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision-card" aria-label="Working approach">
        <span>Evidence</span><i>→</i><span>Trade-offs</span><i>→</i><strong>Decision</strong><i>→</i><strong>Value</strong>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="proof-grid" aria-label="Professional summary">
      <article><strong>15+ years</strong><span>Government, public investment, consultancy and academia</span></article>
      <article><strong>Core focus</strong><span>Public value · Stewardship · Investment · Place</span></article>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" id="work">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Selected work</span><h2>Economics that survives contact with institutions.</h2></div>
        <p>The technical answer is rarely the whole answer. My work connects evidence, incentives, delivery capacity and accountability so analysis can support better real-world decisions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="work-grid">
        <article><small>01 · Capital allocation</small><h3>Public investment and impact</h3><p>Helped establish and scale the impact function at the UK Infrastructure Bank, now the National Wealth Fund, supporting infrastructure decisions across energy, transport and digital investment.</p><div class="tags"><span>Impact</span><span>Resilience</span><span>Risk and return</span></div></article>
        <article><small>02 · Institutions and delivery</small><h3>Place and financial sustainability</h3><p>Work spanning place, funding, financial sustainability and analytical strategy: understanding additional value, local capacity constraints and responsible transitions.</p><div class="tags"><span>Place</span><span>Prevention</span><span>Transitions</span></div></article>
        <article><small>03 · Decision architecture</small><h3>Public value and value for money</h3><p>Appraisal, economic analysis, impact assessment and public-sector reform—turning imperfect evidence into decisions that are defensible, deliverable and alert to implementation.</p><div class="tags"><span>Appraisal</span><span>VfM</span><span>Implementation</span></div></article>
        <article class="featured-project"><small>04 · Evidence project</small><h3><a href={`${base}projects/rewiring-the-state-evidence/`}>Rewiring the State</a></h3><p>Given the direction of travel toward greater devolution and integration, this evidence project asks what decision-makers need to manage actively if the expected benefits are to materialise—and where confidence in the evidence is stronger or weaker.</p><div class="tags"><span>39 sources</span><span>International evidence</span><span>Active benefits management</span></div><a class="project-link" href={`${base}projects/rewiring-the-state-evidence/`}>Explore the evidence <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section ideas" id="ideas">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Ideas and research</span><h2>What sits between evidence and outcomes?</h2></div>
        <p>I am interested in the institutional layer that standard models often compress: capability, incentives, path dependence, implementation and practical trade-offs.</p>
      </div>
      <blockquote>“Expected benefits are hypotheses to manage, not assumptions to bank.”<footer>A working principle for public investment, reform and innovation</footer></blockquote>
      <div class="question-grid">
        <article><small>Question 01</small><h3>When does “what works” stop being enough?</h3><p>Evidence has to travel through a theory of change, lead indicators and implementation—not be treated as a passive label.</p></article>
        <article><small>Question 02</small><h3>What conditions make reform work?</h3><p>Capability, incentives, accountability and sequencing often determine whether plausible mechanisms become realised benefits.</p></article>
        <article><small>Question 03</small><h3>How should expected benefits be managed?</h3><p>Good stewardship means testing assumptions early, tracking leading indicators, adapting when mechanisms fail and protecting value when benefits take time to emerge.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" id="experience">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Experience</span><h2>Across policy, capital and practice.</h2></div>
        <p>A career built across different institutional vantage points, with a consistent focus on consequential choices under constraint.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline">
        <article><small>Now</small><h3>UK Civil Service · Government Economic Service · DCMS</h3><p>Strategic economics spanning place, financial sustainability, analytical strategy and evidence-informed decision-making.</p></article>
        <article><small>Public investment</small><h3>UK Infrastructure Bank · now National Wealth Fund</h3><p>Helped establish and scale the impact function, connecting impact, resilience and public value with investment risk and return.</p></article>
        <article><small>Consultancy and government</small><h3>Simetrica–Jacobs · EY · HM Treasury</h3><p>Economic appraisal, impact assessment, regulation, value for money and public-sector strategy, alongside experience in major delivery departments.</p></article>
        <article><small>Academic foundation</small><h3>University of Nottingham</h3><p>PhD in Economics and Visiting Fellow at Nottingham University Business School, bridging academic evidence, policy and practice.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section about" id="public-service">
      <div class="section-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">Public service and stewardship</span><h2>Evidence is a public trust.</h2></div><p>For me, public-service economics is ultimately about stewardship: using evidence carefully, being candid about uncertainty, making trade-offs visible and treating public resources as something held on behalf of others.</p></div>
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div><p>The Civil Service values of integrity, honesty, objectivity and impartiality are part of the standard by which I think good public analysis should be judged. That means distinguishing evidence from judgement, presenting uncertainty and findings that cut in different directions, and remaining alert to who gains, who bears risk and who has access to opportunity.</p><p>My professional motivation is public value: better decisions, fairer access to opportunity and institutions that learn from evidence as it develops.</p></div>
        <div class="principles"><article><strong>Integrity in analysis.</strong><span>Evidence should be presented accurately, including uncertainty, limitations and findings that cut in different directions.</span></article><article><strong>Objectivity needs uncertainty.</strong><span>Confidence, limitations and competing explanations belong in the answer.</span></article><article><strong>Fairness is part of value.</strong><span>Who benefits, who pays and who can participate are economic questions, not afterthoughts.</span></article></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section about" id="about">
      <div class="section-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">About · He/Him</span><h2>How can institutions make better decisions under uncertainty?</h2></div></div>
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div><p>The hardest challenges facing governments, investors and organisations are rarely purely technical. More often, they are problems of incentives, institutions and implementation.</p><p>My work sits at the intersection of economics, investment, public value and strategy. My experience of neurodiversity has reinforced my belief that high-performing organisations combine cognitive diversity, analytical rigour and accountability for delivery.</p></div>
        <div class="principles"><article><strong>Evidence is necessary, not sufficient.</strong><span>Institutions still need judgement, learning and accountability.</span></article><article><strong>Implementation is part of strategy.</strong><span>Capacity and incentives shape what is genuinely possible.</span></article><article><strong>Trade-offs should be visible.</strong><span>Plural value requires explicit choices, not false precision.</span></article></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="contact" id="contact">
      <span class="eyebrow">Professional exchange</span><h2>Connect on evidence, economics and public value.</h2><p>I welcome professional and academic exchange across government, universities, industry and civil society. This site is for public learning and professional exchange, not commercial solicitation.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/philipbball/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a><a class="button secondary" href={`${base}publications/`}>Explore research</a></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <style>
    .home-shell{width:min(var(--site-width),calc(100% - 2rem));margin:0 auto;padding:0 0 5rem}.hero{min-height:70vh;display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.25fr) minmax(280px,.75fr);gap:3rem;align-items:center;padding:clamp(3rem,8vw,7rem) 0 4rem}.eyebrow{text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.15em;font-size:.72rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--accent)}.hero h1{font-size:clamp(3.3rem,8vw,7.2rem);line-height:.89;letter-spacing:-.065em;margin:.7rem 0 1.7rem}.hero h1 em{font-style:normal;color:var(--accent)}.hero-copy>p{font-size:clamp(1.05rem,2vw,1.28rem);line-height:1.65;max-width:760px;color:var(--text-secondary)}.hero-actions{display:flex;gap:.8rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:1.6rem}.button{display:inline-block;padding:.85rem 1.1rem;border-radius:12px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:750;border:1px solid var(--border)}.button.primary{background:var(--accent);color:white;border-color:var(--accent)}.button.secondary{background:var(--bg-secondary)}.decision-card{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:1rem;padding:1.5rem;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:24px;background:var(--bg-secondary);box-shadow:0 18px 50px rgba(20,32,51,.08)}.decision-card i{color:var(--accent);font-style:normal}.decision-card strong{color:var(--accent)}.proof-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;border-top:1px solid var(--border);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.proof-grid article{padding:1.2rem;display:grid;gap:.25rem}.proof-grid article+article{border-left:1px solid var(--border)}.proof-grid span{color:var(--text-secondary)}.content-section{padding:clamp(4rem,8vw,7rem) 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.section-intro{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.4fr .8fr;gap:2rem;align-items:end;margin-bottom:2rem}.section-intro h2,.contact h2{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4.8rem);letter-spacing:-.045em;margin:.4rem 0}.section-intro>p{color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.65}.work-grid,.question-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:1rem}.work-grid article,.question-grid article{padding:1.4rem;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:20px;background:var(--bg-secondary)}small{color:var(--accent);font-weight:750;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em}.work-grid h3,.question-grid h3{font-size:1.45rem}.work-grid p,.question-grid p,.timeline p,.contact p{color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.65}.tags{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem;margin-top:1rem}.tags span{padding:.3rem .55rem;border-radius:999px;border:1px solid var(--border);font-size:.75rem}.ideas blockquote{font-size:clamp(1.8rem,4vw,3.8rem);line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:2.5rem 0;padding-left:1.2rem;border-left:5px solid var(--accent)}blockquote footer{font-size:.85rem;letter-spacing:0;color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:1rem}.timeline{display:grid}.timeline article{display:grid;grid-template-columns:150px 1fr 1.2fr;gap:1.5rem;padding:1.5rem 0;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}.timeline h3{margin:0}.about-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem}.about-grid p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.75}.principles{display:grid;gap:.75rem}.principles article{padding:1rem;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;background:var(--bg-secondary);display:grid;gap:.35rem}.principles span{color:var(--text-secondary)}.contact{padding:clamp(4rem,8vw,7rem) 0}.contact p{font-size:1.1rem;max-width:850px}.navbar{backdrop-filter:blur(16px)}
    .work-grid .featured-project{grid-column:1/-1;border-top:4px solid var(--accent)}.featured-project h3 a,.project-link{color:var(--text-primary);text-decoration:none}.featured-project h3 a:hover,.project-link:hover{color:var(--accent)}.project-link{display:inline-block;margin-top:1rem;font-weight:800}
    @media(max-width:900px){.hero,.section-intro,.about-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.hero{min-height:auto}.work-grid,.question-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.timeline article{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.45rem}.proof-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.proof-grid article+article{border-left:0;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}}
    @media(max-width:520px){.hero h1{font-size:clamp(2.8rem,15vw,4.2rem)}.decision-card{grid-template-columns:1fr auto}.hero-actions .button{width:100%;text-align:center}}
  </style>
</BaseLayout>